WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.485
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.550
HOST: I'll be very brief.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:16.110
I promise.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.630
On behalf of the Council
for the arts at MIT,

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.890
I'd like to welcome all
of you here this evening.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:30.220
As Maureen said, the 1998
Eugene McDermott award

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.390
was presented to Junot
Diaz last November.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.840
And we're very fortunate to
have him here with us this week

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.730
for a short residency at MIT.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.800
Junot Diaz was born in
the Dominican Republic

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.340
and moved to the United
States at the age of seven.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.250
Much of his fiction
draws on this experience,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.180
as he writes about the clash
and confluence of cultures,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.980
and that sense of
dislocation, which

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.510
is so much a part of the
immigrant experience.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.280
His stories take
us back and forth

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.560
between the streets
of Santa Domingo

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.200
and the neighborhoods of New
Jersey where he and his family

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.810
settled.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.260
Junot Diaz writes with a
jeweler's eye for detail

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.170
and an unsparing, honest voice.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:23.180
He confronts issues of racism
and ethnic identity, sexuality,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.810
and family turmoil,
while taking issue

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:30.160
with the empty promises
of the American dream.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.480
His first book of fiction titled
Drown received unanimous praise

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.200
from critics when it
was published in 1996.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.160
He currently teaches creative
writing at Syracuse University

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.650
and has had his stories
selected for the best American

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:49.870
short stories, both
in 1996 and in 1999.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.100
Just a week ago, he was awarded
a Guggenheim fellowship.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.140
So he has lots to celebrate.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.360
Please join me in
welcoming Junot Diaz.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:05.602
