It clears your
head of any rules.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:08.900
Loretta's new
boyfriend was Italian

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.270
and worked on Wall Street.

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:14.780
When she told me about him,
we were still going out.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:18.770
We were on the promenade, and
she said to me, I like him.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.110
He's a hard worker.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:24.320
And no amount of heart leather
could stop something like that

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.370
from hurting.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:30.150
After one of their showers,
boyfriend never came back,

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.400
no phone calls, no nothing.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.740
She called a lot of her
friends, ones she hadn't

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.480
spoken to in the longest.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.370
I survived through my boys.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.320
I didn't have to
call out for help.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.730
It was easy for them to
say forget her sellout ass.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.860
That's not the sort
of woman you need.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:48.480
Look how light you are.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.870
No doubt she was already
shopping for the lightest.

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:55.560
Mostly, girlfriend spent her
time crying, either in bathroom

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.000
or in front of the TV.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:00.750
And I spent my time listening
and calling around for a job,

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.960
or smoking, or drinking
a bottle of rum

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.750
and two sixes of
Presidente a week.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.110
And one night, one night I
got the [SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:14.550
to ask her out for cafe, which
was mighty manipulative of me.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.340
She hadn't had much human
contact the whole month

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:20.610
except with the delivery guy
from the Japanese restaurant,

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:23.280
a Colombian dude I
always said hi to.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.195
So what the hell was
she going to say to me?

00:14:25.195 --> 00:14:25.695
No?

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.530
She seemed glad to hear my name.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:32.940
And when she threw
open the door,

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:36.330
I was surprised to see her
looking smart and watchful.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.310
She said she'd be right up.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:41.280
And when she sat down across
from me at the kitchen table,

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.850
she had on a makeup and
a rose gold necklace.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:46.950
You have a lot more
light in your apartment

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:51.030
than I do, she said, which
was a nice call about all I

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:52.680
had in the apartment was light.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.500
I played
[SPEAKING SPANISH] for her.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.090
And then we drank a pot of cafe.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:08.250
El pico, I told her,
nothing but the best.

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.350
We didn't have
much to talk about.

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:12.750
She was depressed and tired.

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.810
And I had the worst
gas of my life.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:20.040
Twice, I had to excuse
myself, twice in an hour.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.590
She must have thought
that bizarre as hell.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.290
But both times I came
out of the bathroom

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:28.020
she was staring
deeply into her coffee

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:31.230
the way the fortune tellers
will do back on the island.

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.920
Crying all the time had
made her more beautiful.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:38.640
Grief will do that
sometimes, not for me.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:41.010
Loretta had been
gone now for months,

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.350
and I still look like shit.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:45.600
Having girlfriend in
the apartment only

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.550
made me feel shabbier.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.290
She picked up a cheeb seed
from a crack in the table

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:51.780
and she smiled.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:53.790
Do you smoke, I ask?

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:55.620
It makes me break out, I said.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.840
It makes me sleepwalk.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.120
Honey will stop that, she said.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.130
It's an old Caribbean cure.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:04.100
I had a deal who
would sleepwalk,

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:09.050
and one teaspoon a night
took it out of him.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:12.680
I never tried the honey,
and she never came back.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:16.310
Whenever I saw her on the
stairs, we would trade hi's.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:18.710
But she's never
slowed down to talk,

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:22.280
never gave a smile, or any
other kind of encouragement.

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:24.530
I took that as a hint.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:26.540
At the end of the
month, she got her hair

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:29.480
cut short, no more
straighteners, no more

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.550
science fiction combs.

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:33.320
I liked that, I told her.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:35.550
I was coming back
from the liquor store

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:38.280
and she was on her way
out with a woman friend.

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:39.440
I like that.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.300
It makes you look fierce.

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:42.620
She smiled.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.690
That's exactly what I wanted.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.072
Thank you.

00:16:47.072 --> 00:16:49.552
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:01.938 --> 00:17:04.230
I keep waiting for that
boyfriend to write me a letter,

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.619
you know, on the real though.

00:17:13.865 --> 00:17:15.240
I always think
this takes longer.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:20.310
Then I look at my watch and
realized oh, I must go on.

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:26.780
So I used to go--

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.520
for all you prospective
young writers,

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.682
I used to go see other writers.

00:17:31.682 --> 00:17:33.390
You know, you would
go to their readings.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:37.350
And the majority of them
were absolutely unbearable

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:40.990
because so many of them
would read for 59 minutes.

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:43.020
And you could feel
your beard growing.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.600
So I had this thing.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:49.360
I always promised myself to
keep it as short as possible.

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:51.510
This is a story.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.370
This is something.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.580
I was sort of working on
this cycle of stories.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:58.870
Come on in.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:00.495
I was working on this
cycle of stories.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.930
And it was kind of strange
because, once again, I

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:11.650
was sort of dealing with people
dating and their relationship.

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:16.660
And I was moving through this
life of a fictional Dominican

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:17.230
cat.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.410
And it was sort of
strange because I

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.750
was moving through
all the relationships

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:25.360
this guy had in his entire
life, from the time when

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:28.000
he was in second grade,
and some girl was like,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.040
me and my girlfriend
want to date you,

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:32.590
from the time where
he's sort of telling

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:34.360
his story from his
mother's basement,

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.770
and all his Romeo
days are burnt out,

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:38.270
and he's now living
with his mother,

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.830
you know, like so many men I
know living with their moms.

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:44.230
So he's sort of down
in the basement,

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:45.490
like writing his little thing.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:48.010
And so this comes
out of that section.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:52.340
And it's this-- and it was
weird because in the book,

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:53.638
it's like--

00:18:53.638 --> 00:18:54.180
I don't know.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.410
I'm always just twisting the
knife in brothers in a way

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:58.830
because I'm one of them.

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.980
And it's good to be--

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:02.790
so we're talking about the--

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:05.040
I grew up with Cubans.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.300
And I love that term.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:08.610
I love that, almost
that cultural pattern,

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.860
[SPEAKING SPANISH],,
which so many of them

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:13.070
bring from the island
self-criticism.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:14.430
I adore that shit.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:18.630
So I was writing this thing
about when we were young,

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:20.760
my friends were young,
and they were all--

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:24.150
my friends, we were all
wild and out on white girls.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.350
There was this obsession.

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:26.610
And all the cats
thought they were better

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.350
looking than sisters, you know?

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:31.290
And so much of it was like
weird ass self-hatred.

00:19:34.785 --> 00:19:37.202
Well, maybe some of you don't
know what I'm talking about.

00:19:37.202 --> 00:19:39.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:39.990
But fuck it.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:46.230
So I took this segment out of
that kind of perverse stuff.

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:49.520
And that's where it comes from.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.300
And it's a short called Flaca.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:55.440
And you'll have to bear with
me because not only is only

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.600
it something new, but I'm
also working with a style

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:01.080
where lately I've been like
fragmenting shit, which

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:04.510
means that it makes a lot sense
less sense than it should.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:05.010
Good luck.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:13.390
[? Flaca, ?] your
left eye used to drift

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.150
when you were tired or upset.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.970
It's looking for a way
out, you used to say.

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:21.220
And those days we
saw each other,

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:23.200
it fluttered, and
rolled, and you

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:26.260
had to put your finger
over it to stop it.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:28.930
You were doing this when
I woke up and found you

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.760
on the edge of my chair.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:33.190
You were still in your
teacher's uniform,

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:34.930
but your jacket was off.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:37.390
And enough buttons were
open on your blouse

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.030
to show me the black bra
that I had bought you

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.470
and the freckles on your chest.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:46.600
We didn't know it was the
last days, but we should have.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.700
I just got here, you said.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:51.310
And I looked out where
you'd parked your Saturn.

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:53.680
Go roll up them windows, I said.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:56.290
I'm not going to be
here long, you said.

00:20:56.290 --> 00:20:58.180
Someone's going to steal it.

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:00.730
I'm almost ready to go.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:02.500
You stayed in your
chair, and I knew

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:04.420
better than to move closer.

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:06.550
You had an elaborate
system that you thought

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:08.290
would keep us out of bed.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:10.400
You sat on the other
side of the room,

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.220
you didn't let me
crack your knuckles,

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.830
and you never stayed
more than 15 minutes.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:16.690
It never worked.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:19.150
I brought you guys
some dinner, you said.

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:21.560
I was making lasagna
for my class,

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.330
so I brought the leftovers.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:23.830
My

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:27.280
Room back then
was small and hot.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:29.410
And you never wanted
to be in there.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:32.140
It's like being inside
of a sock, you said.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:35.620
And any time the boys were away,
we slept in their living room

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:37.000
out on the rug.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:39.160
Your long hair was
making you sweat,

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:42.070
and finally, you took your
hand away from your eye.

00:21:42.070 --> 00:21:44.010
You hadn't stopped talking.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.600
Today, I was given
a new student.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:50.140
Her mother told me to
be careful with her

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.910
because she had the sight.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:54.250
And I said, the sight?

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:57.100
And she said, you said the site.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:22:00.320
I asked the senora if the
site helped her in school.

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:02.290
And she said, no, not really.

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:06.580
But it's helped me with
the numbers a few times.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:09.670
I knew I was supposed to laugh.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:13.120
But I stared outside instead
where a mitten-shaped leaf

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:14.980
had stuck to your windshield.

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:16.810
You stood besides me.

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:21.160
When I first saw you in our
Joyce class and then at gym,

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:23.140
I knew I'd call you Flaca.

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:25.380
And if you'd been
Dominican, my neighbors

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.460
would have worried about
you and brought you

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:32.140
plates of food to my door, heaps
of platano and yucca smothered

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:33.940
in quesco frito.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:38.020
Flaca, even though
your name was Veronica,

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:40.300
the boys will be
here soon, I said.

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:42.550
Maybe you should
roll up your windows.

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:45.610
I'm going now, you said,
and you put your hand back

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:47.610
over your eye.

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:52.950
Too, I remember the boys
keeping an eye on me

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:56.310
because they were convinced
that I was going to flip out.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.490
They figured two years ain't
no small thing, even though

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.290
that entire time I
never claimed you.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:05.400
But what was nuts
was that I felt fine.

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:08.730
I felt like summer had
opened up shop inside of me.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.160
And I told the boys
this was the best

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:13.290
decision that I'd ever made.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.440
You can't be fucking with
white girls all your life.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:20.190
And they looked at me like
I had a hole in my head.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.260
After you dropped
me off that night,

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:24.570
the old man and
stinky wanted to know

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:26.740
what the fuck I was thinking.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:30.340
And I said, I'm thinking
we should go out tonight.

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:34.360
I'm thinking I got to
find me a sister soon.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:38.670
Three, I remember
we met in class.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:42.090
You never spoke, but
I did all the time.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:45.090
And once you looked at
me and I looked at you.

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:48.210
And you turned so red,
even the professor noticed.

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.940
You were white trash from
outside of Patterson,

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:53.640
and it showed in your
no fashion sense.

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:55.740
And you dated niggers a lot.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.960
I said, you had
a thing about us.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.000
And you said, angry, no.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.510
I do not.

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:05.430
And I remember you used to offer
me rides home in your Saturn.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.730
And I remember the
third time I accepted.

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:13.780
And our hands touched in the
front seat, little fugitives.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:19.820
Four, it wasn't supposed
to get serious between us.

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:22.400
I can't see us getting
married or nothing.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:25.670
And you nodded your head
and said you understood.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:28.490
Then we fucked so that we
could pretend that nothing

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:30.510
hurtful had just happened.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.600
This was like our
fifth time together.

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:34.610
And you got dressed
in a black sheath

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:36.500
and a pair of Mexican sandals.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.290
And you said I could
call you when I wanted

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.240
but that you wouldn't call me.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:43.890
You have to decide where
and when, you said.

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.780
If you leave it up to me, I
want to see you every day.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:50.030
At least you were honest,
which is more than I can say

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:54.770
for a lot of people, who
pretend to be mad independent

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:56.090
and aren't.

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:58.100
Weekdays, I never called you--

00:24:58.100 --> 00:24:59.630
didn't even miss you.

00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:02.570
I had my boys and my
job to keep me busy.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:05.360
But Friday and Saturday nights
when I didn't meet anybody

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:07.430
at the clubs, I called.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:10.340
Those days, none of us
wanted to go to bed alone.

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:12.120
And I wasn't no different.

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:15.500
We talked until the silences
were long, until finally, you

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.510
asked, do you want to see me?

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:20.330
You made me say yes.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:21.500
I want to see you.

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:24.170
And while I waited for you, I
would tell the boys it's just

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:26.420
sex, you know, nothing more.

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:29.150
And you would come with a
change of clothes and a pan

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:31.340
so you could make us
breakfast, maybe cookies

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:33.080
you have baked for your class.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:35.190
And the boys would
find you in the morning

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:36.440
in one of my shirts.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:38.180
And at first they
didn't complain

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:39.950
because they figured
you would just

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:42.080
go away like the
other white girls who

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:43.550
would pass through the house.

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:45.960
By the time they started
saying something,

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.760
it was late, wasn't it?

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:52.600
Five , you wanted
to go somewhere,

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:54.870
so I took us out to Spruce Run.

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:57.000
We'd both been
there as children.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.650
You could remember the
years, even the months.

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:04.950
But the closest I came to
was back when I was young.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.620
Look at the Queen
Anne's lace, you said.

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.960
You were leaning out the
window in the night air,

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:14.410
and I had my hand on
your back just in case.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:16.980
What did your family
do here, you asked?

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:18.840
I looked at the night water.

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.630
We had barbecues,
Dominican barbecues.

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:25.020
My father didn't know
how to, but he insisted.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:27.390
He would cook up this
red sauce that he'd

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:29.310
splatter on [SPEAKING SPANISH],,
and then he'd

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:32.430
invite complete strangers
over to eat with us.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:33.195
It was horrible.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:39.330
I wore an eye patch when
I was a kid, you said.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:43.380
Maybe we met out here and fell
in love over bad barbecue.

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:46.770
And I said, I doubt it.

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:49.950
And you said, I'm just
saying, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:49.950 --> 00:26:53.490
Maybe 5,000 years
ago we were together.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:57.280
And you said, 5,000 years
ago I was in Denmark.

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:58.900
And I said that's true.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:01.830
And about half of
me was in Africa.

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:03.670
And you said, doing what?

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:05.850
And I said, farming, I
guess, because that's

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.160
what everybody does everywhere.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:10.320
Maybe we were together
some other time.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.720
And I said, I can't think when.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:14.460
You tried not to look at me.

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:16.590
Maybe 5 million years ago.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.260
And I said, people weren't
even people back then.

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:26.190
That night, you lay in bed awake
and listen to the ambulance

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:27.780
tear down our street.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:29.520
You were hot from something.

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.370
Your cheek could've kept
my room warm for a week.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:35.140
And I didn't know how you
stood the heat of yourself,

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:37.080
of your breast, of your face.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.030
I almost couldn't touch you.

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:42.120
And out of nowhere
you said, I love you,

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:44.550
for whatever that's worth.

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:51.280
Six, some nights I can't
sleep, and some nights I

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:53.680
pull on my sneakers and run.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.470
These are the only times
I break two miles, when

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:59.290
there's no traffic and the
halogens turn everything

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:02.380
the color of foil and fire
up every bit of moisture

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:03.870
that's on the cars.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:06.520
I run down and around
the memorial homes

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:10.510
along George Street, past
troop where the Camelot stands

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.310
boarded and burned,

00:28:12.310 --> 00:28:13.660
I stay up entire nights.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:16.260
And when the old man
comes home from UPS,

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.470
I'm noting down the
times the trains arrive

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.080
from Princeton junction.

00:28:20.080 --> 00:28:23.350
You can hear them breaking from
our living room, a [? nash ?]

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:25.190
just south of my heart.

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:28.000
And I think this staying
up means something.

00:28:28.000 --> 00:28:31.010
But the old man
hears me and laughs.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.210
He's a brother who believes
in the practical and hardwood

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:36.250
floors and paperclips.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:38.410
You need another job, he says.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:41.660
And he shows me his beaten
up hands, a good one.

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:43.150
That's what this is about.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:45.490
You need something that
will give you dreams.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:47.530
You can't sleep without dreams.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:51.460
Right now, you're just all out.

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:54.010
Six, last section.

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:54.790
Hmm.

00:28:54.790 --> 00:28:57.940
We went back to
Spruce Run once more.

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:01.570
Do you remember, when the
fights seemed to go on and on,

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:04.360
and every one ended with us
in bed tearing at each other,

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.710
like maybe that could
change anything?

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:09.710
In a couple of months, you'd
be seeing someone else.

00:29:09.710 --> 00:29:10.930
And I would too.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.960
She was no darker than you,
but she watched her panties

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.630
in the shower and had
hair like a sea of little

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:17.620
[SPEAKING SPANISH].

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:20.890
And the first time you saw us
, you turned around and boarded

00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:24.130
a bus I knew you
didn't have to take.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:28.030
That second trip I stood on the
beach and watched you wade out,

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:31.330
watched you rub the lake on
your skinny arms and neck.

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:35.290
Both of us were hungover, and
I didn't want any of me wet.

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:37.870
There's a cure in the
water, as you said.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:40.090
The priest announced
it at service.

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:43.720
You were saving some in a bottle
for your cousin with leukemia

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:45.910
and your aunt with
the bad heart.

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:48.520
You had on a bikini
bottom and a t-shirt.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:51.250
And there was a mist
sifting down over the water

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:52.870
lacing the trees.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:55.270
You went out to your
waist and stopped.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:58.610
I was staring at you, and
you were staring up at me.

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:03.230
And right then it was something
sort of like love, wasn't it?

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:06.770
That night, you came into my
bed, too thin to be believed.

00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:08.420
And when I tried
to kiss you, you

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:10.580
put your hand across my chest.

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:12.020
Wait, you said.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:16.640
Downstairs the boys were
watching TV and screaming.

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:20.600
You let the water dribble out
of your mouth, and it was cold.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:23.930
You reached my knee before you
had the refill from the bottle.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:25.970
I listened to your
breathing, how slight

00:30:25.970 --> 00:30:30.350
it was, and listen to the sound
the water made in the bottle.

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:32.510
You whispered my
full name, and I

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:36.620
remember how the next morning
you were gone, completely gone.

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:39.020
And nothing in my
bed or in my house

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:42.072
could have proven otherwise.

00:30:42.072 --> 00:30:43.280
And that's that for that one.

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.760
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:56.987
Have I done enough?

00:30:56.987 --> 00:30:58.150
AUDIENCE: You've done it.

00:30:58.150 --> 00:31:04.730
DIAZ: OK, it's weird because the
second story is more internal.

00:31:04.730 --> 00:31:07.427
And lately, I've been writing
these more internal things

00:31:07.427 --> 00:31:09.260
that end up being kind
of dull to listen to.

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:14.000
So you've got to
deal with it though.

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:16.570
Do you have any questions?

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:18.820
Oh, couldn't wait.

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:21.157
AUDIENCE: Yeah, couldn't wait.

00:31:21.157 --> 00:31:22.740
Where did you do
your formal training,

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:25.190
and what steps did you take
to get your first election

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.350
of stories [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:28.475
DIAZ: Formal training,
could you elaborate exactly?

00:31:28.475 --> 00:31:31.010
AUDIENCE: Yeah, do an MFA, or--

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:33.140
DIAZ: OK, thank you.

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:34.340
Yeah.

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:37.040
No, thank you for your question.

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:41.390
I had started writing when
I was a junior or senior

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:42.620
in undergraduate.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:44.630
And I was accepted into an MFA.

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:47.750
I applied to five
of them because I

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:49.520
couldn't afford more than five.

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:51.690
And I got denied
everywhere but Cornell,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:53.930
which was funny, because
Cornell was quote,

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:55.880
unquote "good
school" I applied to.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:32:00.110
So it shows you how fucking
arbitrary this shit is.

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:01.190
I didn't kid myself.

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:01.730
I got in.

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:03.800
I used to be like,
I didn't accept--

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:06.360
I didn't get accepted to
Vermont Mount College, you know?

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:12.350
And I went there for my MFA.

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:16.450
But mostly, I was telling
some other students before,

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:18.440
I really wilded out in
my MFA, because mostly I

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:20.610
was a community organizer.

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:23.540
And so when I got
there, I came in.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.790
It was back when Cornell
grad school was still letting

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:27.860
in kids from state schools.

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:31.010
And so they accidentally
let a bunch of us

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:33.530
in from New Jersey who
all knew each other.

00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:36.170
And none of us went to class.

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.560
And we just wilded out for the
Latino and black community.

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:41.240
And so it was only
till my senior year

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:45.474
that I really started
to take it seriously.

00:32:45.474 --> 00:32:48.255
AUDIENCE: And how did you
get your own first collection

00:32:48.255 --> 00:32:49.900
of stories published?

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:50.400
DIAZ: Yeah.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:52.380
I mean, we say it at
home [SPEAKING SPANISH]..

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:54.150
It was just a complete accident.

00:32:54.150 --> 00:32:57.000
I published one story.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:58.620
I had been in graduate school.

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:02.040
After three years, I hadn't
sent in a story anywhere.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:04.920
And I had gone to see my chair.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.450
And she was like--

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:08.772
because if you do
a lot of activism,

00:33:08.772 --> 00:33:10.230
which we'll talk
about in a minute,

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:12.360
you're more than
likely to be despised

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:13.770
by everyone in your department.

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:18.150
And so I had done all this
activism, and one of it

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:21.510
was critiquing the
English department.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:26.382
And so we like really rammed
it to the English department

00:33:26.382 --> 00:33:28.590
because a lot of us were
sitting on these committees,

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:30.690
and so we went berserk on them.

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.190
And what happened
was we forced them

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:34.860
to hire two faculty in
the English department

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:37.500
on doing US Latino studies.

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:38.890
We just screwed them.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:40.880
We got Helena Maria
Viramontes in there,

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:43.020
and we got somebody
in the PhD program.

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:45.150
And so it was really
a big success for us.

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:48.690
And then I went to my chair
to get her to sign off on me,

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:50.610
and she was like,
I'm not signing it.

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:52.780
Fuck you.

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:55.620
And then I knew I had to leave
because if they don't sign off,

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:57.180
you might as well go home.

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:58.230
So I was on my--

00:33:58.230 --> 00:33:59.640
I said, fuck, I'm doomed.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:02.340
And I hadn't sent
in any stories out,

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:03.930
and so I sent my
first story out.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:05.190
And it was like--

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:08.183
talk about ancestrals
looking out

00:34:08.183 --> 00:34:09.600
because the first
story I sent out

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.717
was accepted and published
by Story Magazine,

00:34:12.717 --> 00:34:14.550
and I was telling the
other students before.

00:34:14.550 --> 00:34:17.370
They were like-- and Story was
like, do you have another one?

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:19.703
And I sent them another one,
and they were like, oh, OK,

00:34:19.703 --> 00:34:20.880
we like this one, too.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:23.400
And then I was
telling the students,

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:25.650
another class of students.

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:29.190
I was like, OK, so I've made
all of $500 on two stories.

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:32.070
I got to go home now, and
what ended up happening was

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:32.840
I had gone home.

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:35.770
I was making photocopies,
and a few months into that,

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:36.810
and an agent called.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.697
And she was like, oh,
I like your stories.

00:34:38.697 --> 00:34:39.780
I'd like to represent you.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:40.636
And I was like, OK.

00:34:40.636 --> 00:34:42.219
She's like, you don't
want to meet me?

00:34:42.219 --> 00:34:43.500
I'm like, not really.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.120
I'm desperate.

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:49.949
So and then a few months later
after that, she was like--

00:34:49.949 --> 00:34:51.636
oh, by the way, she
called me at work.

00:34:51.636 --> 00:34:52.469
They used to think--

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:54.136
I was working at
Pfizer Pharmaceuticals.

00:34:54.136 --> 00:34:56.420
They used to think I
didn't speak English.

00:34:56.420 --> 00:34:57.420
I was so sullen.

00:34:57.420 --> 00:34:58.860
I wouldn't speak to anybody.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:02.010
They would just give
me photocopies to make.

00:35:02.010 --> 00:35:04.220
So I was making photocopies,
and homegirl called me.

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:06.000
She was like, oh, I
just sold your book.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:07.530
Quit.

00:35:07.530 --> 00:35:08.560
And so that was that.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:10.320
It sounds like a
fancy, funny story,

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:14.280
but what's funny about
that story is that I

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:17.310
don't fucking kid myself.

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:19.620
It's like the lottery
because every one of us

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:22.500
who make it like this, that
actually means a thousand

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:25.660
other young writers of
color didn't make it

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:28.960
because it's like a selection
process which definitely

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:31.380
doesn't favor us.

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.630
So my friends-- we were all
like, yeah, this is great.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:35.100
It's nice to eat,
but at the same time,

00:35:35.100 --> 00:35:37.433
we knew every one of us would
get one of these positions

00:35:37.433 --> 00:35:39.200
locks out another 20 people.

00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:42.660
And that's not something
to be all cheery about.

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:45.360
Next question.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:47.350
Right here.

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:50.440
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] race
and beauty in the Dominican

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:57.630
Republic [INAUDIBLE]
What affect did

00:35:57.630 --> 00:36:01.480
that have [INAUDIBLE]
race [INAUDIBLE] beauty,

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:03.410
and how do you work
it out in writing?

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:05.300
DIAZ: Do we have any
other Dominicans here?

00:36:05.300 --> 00:36:06.480
Raise your hand.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:06.980
All right.

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:08.563
Could you answer
that question for me?

00:36:10.615 --> 00:36:11.240
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:14.510
DIAZ: Yeah, why don't you try?

00:36:14.510 --> 00:36:16.590
AUDIENCE: Well, I guess
this is how I see it,

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:17.840
being from New Jersey and all.

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:23.470
Going back and forth from
the island to Jersey,

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:27.230
definitely got mixed messages
about what beauty meant.

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:29.530
My hair, for example--

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:31.920
[INAUDIBLE] but I
couldn't wear it like this

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:38.010
because then I was [SPANISH],,
and I didn't look presentable.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:40.430
Back in Jersey,
back home, I could

00:36:40.430 --> 00:36:43.990
spend a day or two washing it
and leaving it kinky and curly,

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:44.790
and it was OK.

00:36:44.790 --> 00:36:48.545
It was all right as long
as I kept it wrapped up.

00:36:48.545 --> 00:36:53.410
But in DR, they're very strict,
just to use that as a metaphor

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:57.840
and just as a very symbolic
view of how women are perceived.

00:36:57.840 --> 00:37:02.900
In DR, if I were to leave the
house and not straighten it,

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:05.232
that would look
bad on the family.

00:37:05.232 --> 00:37:06.690
So it was just a
different way of--

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:08.857
AUDIENCE: Straighten your
hair, like as in no curls?

00:37:08.857 --> 00:37:10.352
DIAZ: Mm-hmm.

00:37:10.352 --> 00:37:11.310
There was another hand.

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:13.770
Does somebody else
want to try that one?

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:15.400
No?

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:16.960
AUDIENCE: They're
all passing, man.

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:17.960
DIAZ: Yeah.

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:22.580
I mean, I think
Santo Domingo isn't

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:25.520
very different from some
of the communities we

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:26.810
have in the United States.

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:30.160
Negrophobia is just
absolutely rampant,

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:32.710
and I think that people
don't like to talk about it.

00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:36.820
But I mean, the
level of self-hatred

00:37:36.820 --> 00:37:38.980
that I encounter
in Santo Domingo

00:37:38.980 --> 00:37:42.680
has a perfect analog
in the United States,

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:43.740
has a perfect analog.

00:37:43.740 --> 00:37:47.190
So when I hear a lot of people
trying to other the Caribbean

00:37:47.190 --> 00:37:49.240
and say, things are
worse in the Caribbean,

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:52.360
I'm always like,
worse than what?

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:55.270
What the fuck are
you talking about?

00:37:55.270 --> 00:37:57.210
It's such a weird question
because it's like,

00:37:57.210 --> 00:37:58.140
worse than what?

00:37:58.140 --> 00:37:59.680
What's your standard?

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:01.380
And so I know that when--

00:38:01.380 --> 00:38:05.070
it was intense because when
we were in Santo Domingo,

00:38:05.070 --> 00:38:06.000
there was like--

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.760
I mean, in my community where
I grew up, I grew up in a--

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:11.190
I mean, my name was a
testament to that I grew up

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:12.963
in a Dominican and
Haitian community,

00:38:12.963 --> 00:38:14.880
and I was probably the
lightest skinned person

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:17.730
they'd seen in 500 years.

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:20.220
They were so excited
they produced me.

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:23.430
Everybody was just like, my
goodness, we worked so hard.

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:28.630
And it was very clear.

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:31.530
It was so very clear.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:33.930
It was just so very clear
on the island, the things

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:37.200
that were attributed to us.

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:39.150
It didn't matter how
fucked up ugly I was.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:41.850
People were like, oh,
[? tan ?] [? cute. ?] I'm like,

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:43.170
you're bugging.

00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:44.910
But then we came to
the United States,

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.800
and we noticed
something even worse

00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:50.430
is that the United States--

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:55.200
the United States, I guess,
their racial measurements

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:58.110
are at the millimeter
wave so that,

00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:01.112
for example, my older
brother is lighter than me.

00:39:01.112 --> 00:39:03.570
And when we went to school,
people used to throw shit at me

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:05.280
and boo me, and my
brother would come in.

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:07.980
They'd be like, oh,
he's got straight hair

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:09.930
and no signs of negritude.

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:12.450
So I wasn't sure what
the difference was.

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:23.780
Well, I mean, I kind of
got lucky because, I mean,

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.100
I grew up with a lot of sisters,
like women in my family,

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:28.810
and they were real
good at bashing it out

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:30.007
of us in our heads.

00:39:30.007 --> 00:39:32.590
So by the time I got to writing,
I had already, quote unquote,

00:39:32.590 --> 00:39:35.770
"resolved these issues,"
most of them in my head.

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:40.910
And so what I tend to do in my
work is just torture people who

00:39:40.910 --> 00:39:41.410
haven't.

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:50.860
Oh no, it's a whole
crew of evil Dominicans,

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:52.690
people who I absolutely adore.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:58.238
Another question?

00:39:58.238 --> 00:40:02.215
AUDIENCE: [? I ?] [? see a ?]
[INAUDIBLE] between your work

00:40:02.215 --> 00:40:05.400
and that of Piri Thomas in
Down These Mean Streets.

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:10.870
And it's like that whole urban
Latino American experience.

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:13.785
Did you have any influences
from Latino American authors,

00:40:13.785 --> 00:40:16.270
and if so, who?

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:18.130
DIAZ: Yeah, I
mean, well, for me,

00:40:18.130 --> 00:40:21.970
I mean, Piri Thomas's Down
These Mean Streets is like--

00:40:21.970 --> 00:40:24.280
it's one of those
founding texts, right?

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:28.060
And it was definitely one of the
earliest Caribbean Latino texts

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:31.275
to like do some good things
like talk about race,

00:40:31.275 --> 00:40:32.470
et cetera, et cetera.

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:35.860
But there was also some really
like wild, fucked-up shit,

00:40:35.860 --> 00:40:38.620
like unreconstructed--

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:44.830
like just unquestioning
misogyny and just wildness.

00:40:44.830 --> 00:40:49.370
And so it was a real
important text for me to read,

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:52.150
but I was much more affected
by a lot of the women writers

00:40:52.150 --> 00:40:56.200
like, Cristina García and Sandra
Cisneros and people who were

00:40:56.200 --> 00:40:57.040
about 50 times--

00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.530
I was telling another class
[INAUDIBLE] us writers always

00:40:59.530 --> 00:41:00.640
pick--

00:41:00.640 --> 00:41:03.310
well, some of us pick
better parents than we are.

00:41:03.310 --> 00:41:07.650
And so for me, it was like, Toni
Morrison was a huge influence.

00:41:07.650 --> 00:41:12.478
A brother from DC
called Edward P. Jones,

00:41:12.478 --> 00:41:14.770
remarkable short story writer,
probably one of the most

00:41:14.770 --> 00:41:17.177
underrated short-story
writers in the country--

00:41:17.177 --> 00:41:18.760
and he wrote a
short-story book called

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:21.730
Lost in the City, which
the entire book was

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:23.920
set in Washington, DC.

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:28.210
And it was just remarkable where
he organized this collection

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:31.060
around DC
geographically, and that

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:35.830
was just so dope because most of
the kids I knew from New Jersey

00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:39.160
didn't want to use New
Jersey as background

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:45.883
for anything because New
York has a way of erasing.

00:41:45.883 --> 00:41:47.300
Everybody I know
from New Jersey--

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:48.720
they live one week
in New Jersey.

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:50.345
They're like, yeah,
I'm from the Bronx.

00:41:50.345 --> 00:41:53.150
I'm like, oh, nigga, please.

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:54.590
What are you talking about?

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:56.480
I saw you at
Spotswood, you clown.

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:02.140
Other questions?

00:42:02.140 --> 00:42:04.640
AUDIENCE: If you want to get
to some of the political issues

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:07.210
in New York and New Jersey,
they just had [INAUDIBLE] about

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:11.527
profiling, stopping black and
Latino cars on the turnpike,

00:42:11.527 --> 00:42:13.860
and [? they ?] [? fired ?]
[? somebody. ?] Do these kind

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:15.830
of things come up in your
writing or affect your writing

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:17.538
or feel like talking
about some of those?

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:25.550
DIAZ: I mean, I was actually
interested in asking like--

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:27.410
we were talking earlier
