00:44:48.670 --> 00:44:51.360
AUDIENCE: How do you
[? like ?] teaching?

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:54.000
DIAZ: I mean, I always felt
a certain missionary zeal

00:44:54.000 --> 00:45:00.720
about teaching because we used
to gripe all the time about not

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:04.860
having any professors of
color, and so for me, that's

00:45:04.860 --> 00:45:08.550
really important to get in
there and work my students

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:10.905
and not just be
in the classroom,

00:45:10.905 --> 00:45:13.008
but do organizing work.

00:45:13.008 --> 00:45:14.550
And that's cool,
and getting students

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:17.010
prepared for grad school.

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:19.350
I mean, our big thing is to
get our undergrads prepared

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:21.300
for grad school.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.300
When we were grad
students at Cornell,

00:45:24.300 --> 00:45:26.130
each year, the grad
students, the Latino

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:28.500
and black grad students
dedicated themselves

00:45:28.500 --> 00:45:31.080
to producing 10 grad
students every year.

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:34.140
So we placed 10 students
in grad schools,

00:45:34.140 --> 00:45:36.850
and that was just shit
we did on the side.

00:45:36.850 --> 00:45:39.720
And I think that's so important
because one of the failings--

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:44.450
I don't want to sound too
much like I drink too much,

00:45:44.450 --> 00:45:49.240
but I feel like, if anything,
the failings of some

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:51.550
of the movements that came
out of the '60s and '70s

00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:55.480
was some of the weird
ownership issues that came out

00:45:55.480 --> 00:45:57.940
where mentorship is a problem.

00:45:57.940 --> 00:46:00.610
You tend not to see really good
mentorship from one generation

00:46:00.610 --> 00:46:02.500
to the next.

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:03.730
You don't.

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:05.117
I didn't see it.

00:46:05.117 --> 00:46:07.450
I didn't see a lot of these
older activists reaching out

00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:10.090
to young people to have
these young people train

00:46:10.090 --> 00:46:12.340
them to replace them.

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:14.440
What they wanted were followers.

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:16.960
They weren't training
anybody to replace anybody,

00:46:16.960 --> 00:46:19.950
and so that's what I think
teaching is really important.

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:21.430
And I find it to
be like-- we need

00:46:21.430 --> 00:46:25.178
to place people in grad
school and other things.

00:46:25.178 --> 00:46:27.220
There's people who aren't
grad-school orientated.

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:30.130
We need to place them in really
good community jobs and get

00:46:30.130 --> 00:46:32.440
people going, and we got
to-- the attrition rate

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:33.190
at universities--

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:35.170
I don't know how the Latino and
black student attrition rate

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.060
is here, but when
I was at Cornell,

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:40.580
it was like the
highest in the country.

00:46:40.580 --> 00:46:42.630
You know what attrition rate is?

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:43.560
Y'all bugging.

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:47.040
You're supposed to be
the smart students.

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:51.180
Attrition rate is when
students drop out.

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:53.190
So at Cornell, we
had-- the Latino

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:55.290
and black students were
dropping out of Cornell

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:58.650
with an average
grade point of a C,

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:00.330
so that meant they
weren't failing out.

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:02.400
They were cold bolting.

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:04.440
They were just breaking
loose, and so we

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.380
were trying to figure
out ways to stop that,

00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:09.620
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:11.323
[INAUDIBLE] some--
[? go ?] [? ahead. ?]

00:47:11.323 --> 00:47:12.440
AUDIENCE: [? You ?]
[? may have ?] addressed

00:47:12.440 --> 00:47:15.095
[INAUDIBLE] somewhere else, but
I'm curious about your story

00:47:15.095 --> 00:47:16.820
"Ysrael."

00:47:16.820 --> 00:47:20.250
I'm curious about--
is that a common name?

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:21.695
DIAZ: Yeah.

00:47:21.695 --> 00:47:24.070
AUDIENCE: So I was wondering
why you named your character

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:25.910
Ysrael, and I also
wondered if you

00:47:25.910 --> 00:47:29.570
would speak to some of the
American and Dominican tension

00:47:29.570 --> 00:47:32.000
in that story.

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:34.310
I was curious about
Yunior, since it

00:47:34.310 --> 00:47:36.320
didn't strike me that--

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:37.430
I don't speak Spanish.

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:39.860
I'm sorry, so I don't
know whether Yunior

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:44.235
is a common Spanish nickname
or affectionate name or--

00:47:44.235 --> 00:47:46.360
DIAZ: Yeah, half of Santo
Domingo is called Yunior,

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:52.290
and Ysrael is a [INAUDIBLE]--

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:56.450
Ysrael's one of my boys back in
Santo Domingo who drives a cab,

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:58.910
and he was always like, yo,
put me in a fucking story, kid.

00:48:01.706 --> 00:48:04.700
I was like, no doubt.

00:48:04.700 --> 00:48:06.260
Yeah.

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:09.840
Yeah, so it was cool,
though, because I'd

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:12.310
gone to Santo Domingo for
part of the book tour,

00:48:12.310 --> 00:48:14.428
and we had a radio interview.

00:48:14.428 --> 00:48:16.470
And if you think this
country has class problems,

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:19.360
Santo Domingo is ruled
by oligarchical elites,

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:21.300
so you can't imagine the
class problems there.

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:23.850
So we were on the radio.

00:48:23.850 --> 00:48:25.560
We were wilding
out on the radio,

00:48:25.560 --> 00:48:28.320
and all the ex-commies
and left-wing people

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:29.520
were calling up.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:33.240
And they were just like,
yo, fuck the rich people.

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:35.400
And halfway through
all this stuff,

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:39.000
my friend Ysrael called
me from his cab phone,

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:44.200
and he was like, somebody
ate my face in your story.

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:48.270
So I said, come on, now.

00:48:51.690 --> 00:48:52.838
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:48:52.838 --> 00:48:54.546
AUDIENCE: I was
interested in finding out

00:48:54.546 --> 00:49:00.074
more about what you did to get
Latino minority students, let's

00:49:00.074 --> 00:49:03.260
say, [? interested. ?] Can
you tell me a little bit

00:49:03.260 --> 00:49:04.090
more [INAUDIBLE]?

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:04.590
DIAZ: Sure.

00:49:04.590 --> 00:49:06.090
The most important thing is to--

00:49:06.090 --> 00:49:08.630
I mean, we came to
college-- again,

00:49:08.630 --> 00:49:12.030
we were really evil because
we'd been trained at Rutgers,

00:49:12.030 --> 00:49:14.370
which is a very active school.

00:49:14.370 --> 00:49:16.590
And so we came in, and
we had law students.

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:17.902
And we had accounting students.

00:49:17.902 --> 00:49:19.110
We had all sorts of students.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:22.410
And the first thing we dissolved
was this artificial separation

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:24.640
between grad students
and undergrads,

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:26.680
so we just started
wilding out and go

00:49:26.680 --> 00:49:30.790
into the undergrad meetings and
shutting our fucking mouths,

00:49:30.790 --> 00:49:33.280
so we would never speak
during the undergrad meetings.

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:36.210
We just let them run things, and
if they needed any of our help,

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:37.300
we would do it.

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:39.308
And then we would start
offering just services.

00:49:39.308 --> 00:49:41.100
We would start being
like, yo, if anybody's

00:49:41.100 --> 00:49:42.720
interested in grad
school, if anybody

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:44.110
wants to talk about it--

00:49:44.110 --> 00:49:46.110
and the first thing is,
you've got to physically

00:49:46.110 --> 00:49:48.840
be there for your students.

00:49:48.840 --> 00:49:52.410
This old just show up and try to
help them out, that's bullshit.

00:49:52.410 --> 00:49:56.490
I mean, so much of what we
do as activists and flows

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:58.170
across interpersonal
relationships,

00:49:58.170 --> 00:49:59.850
and that was our
most important thing

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:02.490
was to break down those walls.

00:50:02.490 --> 00:50:04.193
And the next thing
we did was we would

00:50:04.193 --> 00:50:06.360
start targeting students
that we thought were really

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:08.460
fucking smart and
shunting them off

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:11.170
to classes of our
peers and friends.

00:50:11.170 --> 00:50:14.970
So my friend Paula was a
PhD in history or English,

00:50:14.970 --> 00:50:17.280
and we would be like, yo,
you got this undergrad kid

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:18.738
running around,
[? and he ?] should

00:50:18.738 --> 00:50:20.590
be in one of your classes.

00:50:20.590 --> 00:50:22.050
And the same thing--

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:23.820
we would just
[? pamp ?] ourselves out

00:50:23.820 --> 00:50:25.170
during our office hours.

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:27.540
All of us would give
our office hours out,

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:30.120
and it would be like
open paper-writing stuff.

00:50:30.120 --> 00:50:31.770
You could bring
any papers through,

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:33.517
and we would work
on them for you.

00:50:33.517 --> 00:50:35.850
So these kids would be funny
because my other kids would

00:50:35.850 --> 00:50:38.130
be waiting to meet
me from a real class,

00:50:38.130 --> 00:50:40.127
and these kids
would just break in.

00:50:40.127 --> 00:50:41.460
And I'd tell my other students--

00:50:41.460 --> 00:50:43.335
I'm like, oh, this is
very important meeting.

00:50:45.790 --> 00:50:48.510
And that really
helps, and I know

00:50:48.510 --> 00:50:49.740
that sounds kind of stupid.

00:50:49.740 --> 00:50:51.840
But I mean, we're
kind of blockheads,

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:54.420
so we weren't trying
to do no master theory.

00:50:54.420 --> 00:50:57.120
Our theory was if we
put ourselves out there,

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:58.620
people are going
to respond to that.

00:51:04.750 --> 00:51:05.383
Yeah.

00:51:05.383 --> 00:51:08.050
AUDIENCE: I was wondering if you
could share some of your dreams

00:51:08.050 --> 00:51:10.690
for the future for
yourself and some of goals

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:16.450
that you had professionally,
I mean, as a writer.

00:51:16.450 --> 00:51:17.000
DIAZ: Yeah.

00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:18.292
I mean, that's a nice question.

00:51:18.292 --> 00:51:19.730
Thank you, but I'm
not so certain.

00:51:19.730 --> 00:51:21.800
I'm like, I want to
want to talk about--

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:23.390
I mean, I came from--

00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:25.667
some of us here--

00:51:25.667 --> 00:51:27.500
my parents and my
grandparents were farmers.

00:51:30.180 --> 00:51:34.190
And so farmers are funny because
they don't plan on anything,

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:37.030
and I think part of
that was given to us.

00:51:37.030 --> 00:51:38.780
I've never seen anybody
in my family dream

00:51:38.780 --> 00:51:41.983
longer than their arm, and
it's not a great thing.

00:51:41.983 --> 00:51:43.400
I mean, I actually
don't recommend

00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:45.680
it highly because,
I mean, it makes

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:47.190
us very blinkered in some ways.

00:51:47.190 --> 00:51:49.070
And I mean, I don't want
to just attribute it

00:51:49.070 --> 00:51:50.153
to some family upbringing.

00:51:50.153 --> 00:51:52.070
It could've just been
[? something ?] [? on ?]

00:51:52.070 --> 00:51:54.710
our side, so I'm really guarded
by that because I'm always

00:51:54.710 --> 00:51:59.990
convinced a meteor is going
to hit my car or something.

00:51:59.990 --> 00:52:02.720
So I mean, one of the things
I just want to keep doing

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:05.600
is just keep doing
organizational work,

00:52:05.600 --> 00:52:13.660
and also, it's important
for any of us who can write

00:52:13.660 --> 00:52:16.140
to do all kinds of writing.

00:52:16.140 --> 00:52:20.050
So all kinds, no matter
what genre it is,

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:21.300
I think we have to tear it up.

00:52:21.300 --> 00:52:23.940
I'm sort of mad at some of
our other writers who are,

00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:25.530
quote unquote,
"literary" haven't just

00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:29.160
tackled every genre they
can possibly tackle,

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:31.560
which means I think
Toni Morrison needs

00:52:31.560 --> 00:52:35.670
to write a horror book
on the real just to mess

00:52:35.670 --> 00:52:38.353
up with these boundaries.

00:52:38.353 --> 00:52:39.770
If I stick around
long enough, I'd

00:52:39.770 --> 00:52:40.978
like to do some of that work.

00:52:44.164 --> 00:52:46.400
AUDIENCE: Speaking
of changing genres,

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:47.910
are you planning
on writing a novel?

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:49.550
Do you have anything in mind?

00:52:49.550 --> 00:52:50.050
DIAZ: Yeah.

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:52.330
AUDIENCE: Tell us about
some of the characters.

00:52:55.010 --> 00:52:55.510
DIAZ: OK.

00:52:57.675 --> 00:52:58.550
AUDIENCE: If you can.

00:52:58.550 --> 00:53:01.910
DIAZ: Yeah, I'm writing
a novel, and I'm not

00:53:01.910 --> 00:53:04.370
going to take the title of
it because I'm absolutely

00:53:04.370 --> 00:53:06.370
convinced by telling you
the title of it, that's

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:07.450
when the meteor hits.

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:12.440
But I'll tell you, the first
section is from a film.

00:53:12.440 --> 00:53:16.820
Do you know this film,
Exterminating Angel?

00:53:16.820 --> 00:53:21.320
Anyway, when I was a kid, my
father was a real film buff

00:53:21.320 --> 00:53:23.870
for this [? hick, ?] and he
used to talk about this film,

00:53:23.870 --> 00:53:25.580
Exterminating Angel.

00:53:25.580 --> 00:53:29.530
And as a kid, you're like,
Exterminating Angel, word up.

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:31.960
You're ready to
see this nonsense.

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:34.770
And then it turns out to be some
Buñuel film where people just

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:42.500
sit around and talk,
so it was not fun.

00:53:42.500 --> 00:53:45.080
And so the first section is
called Exterminating Angel,

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:47.180
and what it's about--

00:53:47.180 --> 00:53:51.320
it's this insane,
absolutely insane book

00:53:51.320 --> 00:53:55.370
about a new minority group.

00:53:55.370 --> 00:53:58.130
There's a new minority
group in the United States,

00:53:58.130 --> 00:54:02.090
and it's actually based
off of an Octavia Butler

00:54:02.090 --> 00:54:07.580
story in Bloodchild, a
really wonderful story where

00:54:07.580 --> 00:54:09.890
she imagines people
with this illness who

00:54:09.890 --> 00:54:11.597
are discriminated against.

00:54:11.597 --> 00:54:13.430
So I just took that one
further and imagined

00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:16.100
an entirely new group,
a minority group,

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:19.400
and everybody calls them
mules because they're sterile.

00:54:19.400 --> 00:54:21.830
And it's just a
real simple story

00:54:21.830 --> 00:54:23.960
about this guy named Lucero.

00:54:27.150 --> 00:54:29.750
He's in an underground
railroad, and he just

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:32.360
spends his time smuggling
mules out of the country.

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:36.380
And it's a United States
now, except that none

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:39.590
of the civil rights
legislation ever passed.

00:54:39.590 --> 00:54:41.960
Instead, there was in '68--

00:54:41.960 --> 00:54:47.440
I was reading these
National Security Agency--

00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:49.545
I was a historian, so
I was into this stuff.

00:54:49.545 --> 00:54:51.920
And I was reading all this
stuff in the National Security

00:54:51.920 --> 00:54:54.580
Agency, and they had
prepared, in '68,

00:54:54.580 --> 00:54:56.900
if there had been mass-scale
rioting, that they would

00:54:56.900 --> 00:55:00.440
incarcerate 10% to 15% of the
black and Latino population

00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:01.970
and put them in
huge prison camps

00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:04.070
and declare a
state of emergency.

00:55:04.070 --> 00:55:06.470
And so I thought these documents
are really interesting,

00:55:06.470 --> 00:55:08.095
finding them, so what
I was going to do

00:55:08.095 --> 00:55:10.610
was imagine a United States
where none of the civil rights

00:55:10.610 --> 00:55:11.660
legislation was passed.

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:14.360
Instead, a state of
emergency was declared,

00:55:14.360 --> 00:55:17.090
and the plan went through.

00:55:17.090 --> 00:55:20.480
And this is 50 years later
when the state of emergency

00:55:20.480 --> 00:55:23.330
continues to be,
and these characters

00:55:23.330 --> 00:55:25.100
are living in this world.

00:55:25.100 --> 00:55:28.250
And it's about this guy named
[? Lucho ?] and his beloved

00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:34.820
girlfriend, [? Yas, ?] and both
of them are sort of conductors.

00:55:34.820 --> 00:55:37.070
And they both hate each
other and are pissed off,

00:55:37.070 --> 00:55:39.320
and it's about their life.

00:55:39.320 --> 00:55:41.545
And what I'm trying
to do is to take--

00:55:41.545 --> 00:55:42.920
I know this sounds
kind of crazy,

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:44.622
but it's kind of
like cut and paste

00:55:44.622 --> 00:55:46.580
because what I'm trying
to do is take stuff out

00:55:46.580 --> 00:55:50.210
of the common culture, like
when people talk about,

00:55:50.210 --> 00:55:52.060
for example, during
the Vietnam War,

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:55.250
experimentations
done on soldiers.

00:55:55.250 --> 00:55:58.130
I love that kind of folk
history, and so what I'm doing

00:55:58.130 --> 00:55:59.630
is I'm just taking
this folk history

00:55:59.630 --> 00:56:02.210
and just pretending
it's all real.

00:56:02.210 --> 00:56:03.480
I know this sounds berserk.

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:06.520
Good luck, plenty of good luck.

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:07.020
I'm sorry.

00:56:07.020 --> 00:56:08.648
I'm going to get this and then--

00:56:08.648 --> 00:56:11.190
AUDIENCE: How much of yourself
do you put in your characters?

00:56:11.190 --> 00:56:14.115
Is there one character that
you can think of that really

00:56:14.115 --> 00:56:15.910
[? represents ?] [? you? ?]

00:56:15.910 --> 00:56:18.940
DIAZ: I, mean I think the
Yunior and the Rafa character

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:23.150
are based on me my brother,
and so the Yunior character

00:56:23.150 --> 00:56:24.880
tends to be the main narrator.

00:56:24.880 --> 00:56:28.840
But I mean, life is stranger
than fiction, so I mean,

00:56:28.840 --> 00:56:31.570
I had to leave a lot of stuff
out and change a lot of things.

00:56:31.570 --> 00:56:34.898
So I wouldn't exactly say
Yunior was me, [? ever. ?]

00:56:34.898 --> 00:56:37.190
I mean, my mother always
answers that question the best

00:56:37.190 --> 00:56:38.940
because when she read
the book in Spanish,

00:56:38.940 --> 00:56:42.000
she was always like, I'm a
lot funnier than this woman.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:44.362
And I'm like, [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:47.705 --> 00:56:48.639
Uh-huh, and--

00:56:48.639 --> 00:56:50.980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:56:50.980 --> 00:56:54.478
DIAZ: No, just no.

00:56:54.478 --> 00:56:55.520
It's like writing poetry.

00:56:55.520 --> 00:57:00.140
It doesn't-- those are really
important forms and I have no

00:57:00.140 --> 00:57:02.630
talent or interest in.

00:57:02.630 --> 00:57:03.440
Thank you, though.

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:06.165
And then back there, and then
here and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:06.165 --> 00:57:07.040
[? get ?] [? this? ?]

00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:10.040
AUDIENCE: Yeah, last little
thing I wanted to ask--

00:57:10.040 --> 00:57:11.770
we're talking about
analogs [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:11.770 --> 00:57:13.740
about physical beauty
and how there's

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:17.520
an analog for that kind of
self-hatred [INAUDIBLE]..

00:57:17.520 --> 00:57:20.270
I was wondering, is there
an analog, do you see,

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:22.696
in a literary sense between--

00:57:22.696 --> 00:57:28.120
in the '60s, there was a black
aesthetic to write [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:30.634
[? of ?] [? artists to ?]
[? write. ?] In the Latino

00:57:30.634 --> 00:57:35.780
community, there's kind of a
Latino aesthetic that's being

00:57:35.780 --> 00:57:39.763
debated or
[? misconceptualized. ?]

00:57:39.763 --> 00:57:42.180
DIAZ: You might know better
than me, in a sense, because I

00:57:42.180 --> 00:57:43.800
think that--

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:46.535
I mean, I think--

00:57:46.535 --> 00:57:47.410
I mean, I don't know.

00:57:47.410 --> 00:57:49.840
I guess what troubles
me most about--

00:57:49.840 --> 00:57:51.590
I mean, you want to
talk about aesthetic--

00:57:51.590 --> 00:57:53.360
I think the debate
that's happening now

00:57:53.360 --> 00:57:55.310
amongst the young
writers I know is

00:57:55.310 --> 00:58:00.050
that there's a small
group of us who

00:58:00.050 --> 00:58:03.470
think it's good to kind
of write unruly stuff

00:58:03.470 --> 00:58:05.900
and to be political
in our lives.

00:58:05.900 --> 00:58:08.420
And then there's another
huge group of young writers

00:58:08.420 --> 00:58:10.980
who-- they just want
to be celebrities.

00:58:10.980 --> 00:58:14.810
It's all about
feeding at the trough.

00:58:14.810 --> 00:58:16.830
It's like a bunch of pigs
eating at the trough,

00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:19.350
and they're like, don't make
too much too much noise.

00:58:19.350 --> 00:58:22.163
You'll interrupt the feeding.

00:58:22.163 --> 00:58:24.080
And so I feel like
[? at least ?] one of the--

00:58:24.080 --> 00:58:30.900
I mean, I think just some old
stuff hasn't been resolved yet.

00:58:30.900 --> 00:58:33.420
I mean, I find so many writers.

00:58:33.420 --> 00:58:35.010
I go to writers
conferences a lot.

00:58:35.010 --> 00:58:37.020
You get invited,
and these writers

00:58:37.020 --> 00:58:39.630
who write very interesting,
complicated things--

00:58:39.630 --> 00:58:41.880
or you think they're very
interesting and complicated.

00:58:41.880 --> 00:58:44.580
And they start
talking, and they say

00:58:44.580 --> 00:58:48.090
some wild, dangerous things.

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:50.610
And you find yourself like--

00:58:50.610 --> 00:58:52.290
I mean, this writers who--

00:58:52.290 --> 00:58:54.900
I was at a conference recently
with a bunch of, quote unquote,

00:58:54.900 --> 00:58:57.990
"very famous" established
black writers,

00:58:57.990 --> 00:59:02.358
and it was painful to behold
because the entire time, these,

00:59:02.358 --> 00:59:04.650
quote unquote, "famous black
writers"-- all they wanted

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:07.840
to talk about was white people.

00:59:07.840 --> 00:59:11.260
And I was just like,
you're an idiot.

00:59:11.260 --> 00:59:13.540
That conversation is played out.

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:15.640
It's about time for
communities to start

00:59:15.640 --> 00:59:17.657
speaking to each other.

00:59:17.657 --> 00:59:19.240
I mean, the black
and Latino community

00:59:19.240 --> 00:59:21.700
hasn't even begun the
dialogs it needs to have.

00:59:21.700 --> 00:59:23.860
I mean, the communities
of color need

00:59:23.860 --> 00:59:25.780
to start dialoguing
with each other

00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:27.660
in ways that have
never happened before.

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:30.820
Like right now, a lot
of these writers just--

00:59:30.820 --> 00:59:32.770
they perform their little
shucking and jiving

00:59:32.770 --> 00:59:34.930
under a white gaze,
and I think that that's

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:38.320
really foolish because
every voice should be equal.

00:59:38.320 --> 00:59:40.180
And if every voice
is equal, then first,

00:59:40.180 --> 00:59:42.520
we need to have a
conversation with each of us.

00:59:42.520 --> 00:59:45.640
And every fifth round, we'll
have a conversation with the,

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:47.400
quote unquote,
"white community."

00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:52.500
But first, I'd like to see some
other conversations occurring.

00:59:52.500 --> 00:59:53.640
Was there a hand there?

00:59:53.640 --> 00:59:54.390
Oh, no, I'm sorry.

00:59:54.390 --> 00:59:55.530
You [INAUDIBLE] and then--

00:59:55.530 --> 00:59:57.573
I'm sure [? I ?] [? dissed ?]
you before, but--

00:59:57.573 --> 01:00:01.430
AUDIENCE: I would like
to know how you prepare

01:00:01.430 --> 01:00:02.970
yourself [INAUDIBLE] to write.

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:06.798
Do you go to certain
places [INAUDIBLE]??

01:00:06.798 --> 01:00:10.011
Is there an emotional
process that you go through?

01:00:10.011 --> 01:00:11.390
What do you do?

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:13.710
DIAZ: Yeah, I mean,
preparation is tough.

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:16.190
I think some of you, by
now, have mastered this kind

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:17.720
of like concentration or focus.

01:00:17.720 --> 01:00:18.620
You need to.

01:00:18.620 --> 01:00:20.570
You wouldn't be here
looking at me right now.

01:00:20.570 --> 01:00:22.707
I mean, and part
of it comes with--

01:00:22.707 --> 01:00:24.290
I mean, I was telling
an earlier class

01:00:24.290 --> 01:00:26.207
is I have to do it first
thing in the morning.

01:00:26.207 --> 01:00:28.390
Once I start interacting
with people, it's finished.

01:00:28.390 --> 01:00:30.750
Then, I just want to have fun.

01:00:30.750 --> 01:00:32.390
I'm a social person.

01:00:32.390 --> 01:00:35.870
But the other thing is that--

01:00:35.870 --> 01:00:38.270
I know this sounds
corny, but it's

01:00:38.270 --> 01:00:41.000
to appreciate the fact that--

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:44.550
I mean, my mother
cleans toilets.

01:00:44.550 --> 01:00:47.910
That's her job, and
so I'm like, OK,

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:50.830
she didn't clean toilets so
that I could write some old

01:00:50.830 --> 01:00:54.450
[? "bleh blah," ?] some
old fucking nonsense.

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:57.660
And I feel like that, for me,
is something that really helps

01:00:57.660 --> 01:00:59.470
me get ready to start writing.

01:00:59.470 --> 01:01:00.970
It's like, think
about the old lady,

01:01:00.970 --> 01:01:04.110
and then I'm like,
OK, time to get going.

01:01:04.110 --> 01:01:05.250
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:07.820 --> 01:01:09.272
Your hand was up.

01:01:09.272 --> 01:01:12.860
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
personal things,

01:01:12.860 --> 01:01:17.450
but when you left to
go do your schooling

01:01:17.450 --> 01:01:20.572
and get your thing done,
how did you deal with maybe

01:01:20.572 --> 01:01:22.530
some guilt that came
across [? from being in ?]

01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:23.420
the neighborhood.

01:01:23.420 --> 01:01:25.550
I know that I've
encountered leaving

01:01:25.550 --> 01:01:29.240
my neighborhood as being
like [? shunning ?] to do

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:31.360
my own thing.

01:01:31.360 --> 01:01:32.250
Does that make sense?

01:01:32.250 --> 01:01:34.382
DIAZ: Yeah, no, it
does make sense.

01:01:34.382 --> 01:01:35.840
But I mean, being
an immigrant, you

01:01:35.840 --> 01:01:40.963
get great lessons in
leaving your community,

01:01:40.963 --> 01:01:42.130
and it's not always leaving.

01:01:44.690 --> 01:01:46.940
I mean, some of us feel no
connections to communities,

01:01:46.940 --> 01:01:48.045
and I'm like, cool.

01:01:48.045 --> 01:01:50.170
But those are not really
the people I'm talking to,

01:01:50.170 --> 01:01:51.100
in a way.

01:01:51.100 --> 01:01:53.680
I feel like if you feel a real
connection to a community--

01:01:53.680 --> 01:01:59.690
again, the community
I belong to isn't

01:01:59.690 --> 01:02:02.150
suffering the sort of
things it's suffering

01:02:02.150 --> 01:02:05.480
and that it's being put on it
so that I could go to school

01:02:05.480 --> 01:02:06.440
and feel real guilty.

01:02:09.170 --> 01:02:11.120
I mean, I know this
sounds grandiose and full

01:02:11.120 --> 01:02:13.910
of shit and idealistic, but
I really believe this stuff.

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:15.410
It's kind of berserk.

01:02:15.410 --> 01:02:16.190
I'm like a sucker.

01:02:16.190 --> 01:02:17.690
I think I'll wake up
one day, but I really

01:02:17.690 --> 01:02:18.500
believe this stuff.

01:02:18.500 --> 01:02:22.820
I was like, they didn't send
me to be a fucking clown,

01:02:22.820 --> 01:02:26.510
and that was a real
serious business for me.

01:02:26.510 --> 01:02:29.090
They were just
like, go and do good

01:02:29.090 --> 01:02:31.220
and don't come back
with some old silliness.

01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:33.980
And that helps, but that
doesn't mean it's not there.

01:02:33.980 --> 01:02:38.842
I know the first year when I was
at Cornell, I was a wild drunk.

01:02:38.842 --> 01:02:40.550
All I did was drink,
drink, drink, drink,

01:02:40.550 --> 01:02:43.280
drink, drink because I
missed home really bad,

01:02:43.280 --> 01:02:47.400
and I felt like I don't even
know what the hell I was doing.

01:02:47.400 --> 01:02:48.950
And so I don't think
it's real easy.

01:02:48.950 --> 01:02:52.907
I think a lot of
us who leave home--

01:02:52.907 --> 01:02:53.990
it's good to have friends.

01:02:59.508 --> 01:03:01.550
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [? I just ?]
[? had a question ?]

01:03:01.550 --> 01:03:05.350
because you put yourself in
a very influential position

01:03:05.350 --> 01:03:08.170
[INAUDIBLE],, and I think
it's great that you can write

01:03:08.170 --> 01:03:12.390
and still manage to
kind of multitask.

01:03:12.390 --> 01:03:15.830
You write, and you're also
galvanizing a lot of people

01:03:15.830 --> 01:03:16.820
and so on.

01:03:16.820 --> 01:03:21.610
And just some of the stuff
that came up before--

01:03:21.610 --> 01:03:24.250
how do you manage to watch
the evening news every night

01:03:24.250 --> 01:03:27.340
and just keep that energy and
keep that idealism that you

01:03:27.340 --> 01:03:29.110
have going?

01:03:29.110 --> 01:03:31.120
I mean, what keeps
you fighting, I guess?

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:33.335
Just because there's
so much when you look

01:03:33.335 --> 01:03:35.660
around [INAUDIBLE] [? down. ?]

01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:38.410
DIAZ: Yeah, but I mean,
what am I really doing?

01:03:38.410 --> 01:03:40.110
Nobody's really beating
me with a chain.

01:03:40.110 --> 01:03:43.770
I think that's
really tough, and I

01:03:43.770 --> 01:03:45.720
don't end up in abuse shelters.

01:03:45.720 --> 01:03:48.300
That's really tough.

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:50.333
I mean, and I'm not really
all that influential.

01:03:50.333 --> 01:03:51.750
Outside of this
room, ain't nobody

01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:55.170
know who the fuck I
am, and most of you

01:03:55.170 --> 01:03:58.980
here don't even know
who the fuck I am, so--

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:05.100
I mean, I make photocopies
for Mumia Abu-Jamal things,

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:08.160
and I go to meetings for
anti-police brutality.

01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:12.480
That doesn't take much time for
me, so I'm pretty much a grunt.

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:14.680
I'm great at making photocopies.

01:04:14.680 --> 01:04:19.100
And so again, you just pick
your small stuff and do them,

01:04:19.100 --> 01:04:21.230
and it's not like I'm
all that successful.

01:04:21.230 --> 01:04:23.720
I should have had
two books out by now.

01:04:23.720 --> 01:04:25.460
If I had dedicated
myself to my writing--

01:04:25.460 --> 01:04:26.750
I have people who--

01:04:26.750 --> 01:04:30.650
we came up together, and they're
going to be happier people.

01:04:30.650 --> 01:04:33.020
Guarantee it because they're
just [? sit ?] at home

01:04:33.020 --> 01:04:36.320
and write, and they're
already on book three.

01:04:36.320 --> 01:04:38.180
And so that's it.

01:04:38.180 --> 01:04:39.260
You feel it.

01:04:39.260 --> 01:04:42.020
You feel it when your friends
send [? you your ?] third book,

01:04:42.020 --> 01:04:44.510
and you're still out in the
street getting spit on by cops.

01:04:44.510 --> 01:04:49.330
You're like, huh, this
doesn't amount to much, so--

01:04:51.523 --> 01:04:53.440
AUDIENCE: Since you're
[? looking ?] [? out ?]

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:55.023
for the first couple
years of college,

01:04:55.023 --> 01:05:02.860
I guess I'm interested in what
it takes to be [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:02.860 --> 01:05:04.250
DIAZ: You know Toni Morrison's--

01:05:04.250 --> 01:05:06.270
I love that influential
essay of hers

01:05:06.270 --> 01:05:09.503
where she talks about how--

01:05:09.503 --> 01:05:10.670
well, I'm going to get Toni.

01:05:10.670 --> 01:05:13.960
But I love Toni, but for
me, it just took somebody--

01:05:16.630 --> 01:05:19.490
somebody set it straight
in a really good way.

01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:21.350
And again, you have to
be personally ready.

01:05:21.350 --> 01:05:24.280
This is not like some
old new-age shit,

01:05:24.280 --> 01:05:27.570
but for me, I was just like
argh, drinking and getting

01:05:27.570 --> 01:05:28.960
into fights.

01:05:28.960 --> 01:05:31.480
I was stupid because I'm
this little nerd kid,

01:05:31.480 --> 01:05:35.330
but I had for years and
years and years been a boxer.

01:05:35.330 --> 01:05:37.843
So I would be like,
hurr hurr, [CLICK],,

01:05:37.843 --> 01:05:39.010
so I was just like an idiot.

01:05:39.010 --> 01:05:42.070
And one day somebody
was just like--

01:05:42.070 --> 01:05:44.078
some woman I never even knew.

01:05:44.078 --> 01:05:45.370
I didn't even know who she was.

01:05:45.370 --> 01:05:50.290
Some sister just walked by, and
she's like, look at this idiot.

01:05:50.290 --> 01:05:53.170
And I was like,
OK, let's do this.

01:05:56.600 --> 01:06:00.528
So I mean, that doesn't
give you much, but for me,

01:06:00.528 --> 01:06:01.320
that's all it took.

01:06:01.320 --> 01:06:04.360
Nothing like being made fun of.

01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:04.860
Why?

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:05.943
Are you having difficulty?

01:06:05.943 --> 01:06:08.430
Are you wilding out, bro?

01:06:08.430 --> 01:06:11.270
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] [? just ?]
[? wondering ?] [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:11.270 --> 01:06:14.480
experience.

01:06:14.480 --> 01:06:17.590
DIAZ: And then,
[? yeah. ?] We're

01:06:17.590 --> 01:06:19.668
about to have last
questions here, guys.

01:06:19.668 --> 01:06:21.103
[INAUDIBLE]

01:06:21.103 --> 01:06:23.020
AUDIENCE: The conference
that you talked about

01:06:23.020 --> 01:06:25.950
and the conferences
that you go to,

01:06:25.950 --> 01:06:30.220
I'm curious as to whether
the focus on white people

01:06:30.220 --> 01:06:36.070
is more of the writers
that came up either before

01:06:36.070 --> 01:06:38.870
or during the Civil Rights
Movement, or [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:38.870 --> 01:06:40.120
DIAZ: They're younger than me.

01:06:40.120 --> 01:06:41.245
AUDIENCE: Younger than you?

01:06:41.245 --> 01:06:43.180
DIAZ: Yeah, well, a
couple were older than me,

01:06:43.180 --> 01:06:46.620
but a bunch of them
were younger than me.

01:06:46.620 --> 01:06:47.550
I mean--

01:06:47.550 --> 01:06:51.093
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
name names [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:51.093 --> 01:06:52.510
DIAZ: The only
reason I don't want

01:06:52.510 --> 01:06:56.150
to name names is not
because [INAUDIBLE]..

01:06:56.150 --> 01:06:58.780
I'll fuck them niggas
up in a minute.

01:06:58.780 --> 01:07:00.200
There's no question about that.

01:07:00.200 --> 01:07:03.430
But it's also, you start
getting into this personal

01:07:03.430 --> 01:07:04.390
"he said, she said."

01:07:04.390 --> 01:07:06.370
It gets away from the
issues, and that's

01:07:06.370 --> 01:07:08.620
the way people tend to boil
it down is that I'm not

01:07:08.620 --> 01:07:10.018
interested in personalities.

01:07:10.018 --> 01:07:11.935
I'm really interested
in some of these issues.

01:07:15.004 --> 01:07:17.740
It's just disappointing
because I always

01:07:17.740 --> 01:07:21.990
hope that the young writers
are going to be beyond kooky,

01:07:21.990 --> 01:07:25.110
but late modern capitalism
has a really good way

01:07:25.110 --> 01:07:28.195
of making sure
everybody's identical.

01:07:28.195 --> 01:07:30.328
I'm like, oh, you
also like money a lot?

01:07:30.328 --> 01:07:30.870
That's great.

01:07:35.070 --> 01:07:36.930
You're real fucking original.

01:07:36.930 --> 01:07:39.260
I mean, whatever.

01:07:39.260 --> 01:07:43.273
OK, I'm going to take one last
question because-- well, I'm

01:07:43.273 --> 01:07:44.940
going to take two and
try to answer them

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:47.315
both because before you leave,
I'm just going to read you

01:07:47.315 --> 01:07:49.930
a page so you can
leave with words.

01:07:49.930 --> 01:07:52.432
I'm going to take your question.

01:07:52.432 --> 01:07:54.640
AUDIENCE: I'm curious, given
your interest in history

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:56.910
and activism, [? do ?]
you think about trying

01:07:56.910 --> 01:07:59.900
to write for a somewhat younger
audience like high school

01:07:59.900 --> 01:08:02.490
kids or younger, even?

01:08:02.490 --> 01:08:06.210
DIAZ: Yeah, I mean, I want to,
but my characters right now

01:08:06.210 --> 01:08:10.560
are so mean to each other
that it really precludes it.

01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:13.350
And it's also some of
the ideas I've been into.

01:08:13.350 --> 01:08:16.140
I think, though, when I get
my first real novel done,

01:08:16.140 --> 01:08:18.000
I think I'll think
about these other things

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:20.640
because I feel like
that's an important genre.

01:08:20.640 --> 01:08:23.939
And when I was a kid, I
mean, the young adult books

01:08:23.939 --> 01:08:26.779
really set me off.

01:08:26.779 --> 01:08:30.040
I can think of some writers who
really had a big impact on me,

01:08:30.040 --> 01:08:32.310
so I'd like to
give a little back.

01:08:32.310 --> 01:08:32.810
Thank you.

01:08:32.810 --> 01:08:34.477
Why would you ask
that question, anyway?

01:08:34.477 --> 01:08:35.880
What were you thinking?

01:08:35.880 --> 01:08:37.720
AUDIENCE: Well, I just think--

01:08:37.720 --> 01:08:42.220
I have read but very little
bit of you [INAUDIBLE]

01:08:42.220 --> 01:08:45.069
and you speak to an audience
that's sitting in front of you,

01:08:45.069 --> 01:08:47.399
mostly, most directly.

01:08:47.399 --> 01:08:49.899
But all of us have already
been formed, as it were.

01:08:49.899 --> 01:08:52.840
The clay is pretty well molded.

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:54.990
You want to challenge
some institutional views,

01:08:54.990 --> 01:08:58.029
your rampant capitalism, you
have to [? tag up ?] [? to ?]

01:08:58.029 --> 01:08:59.569
[? someone ?] younger age.

01:08:59.569 --> 01:09:02.350
That's my view, and I wondered
how you thought about that.

01:09:02.350 --> 01:09:03.950
DIAZ: Yeah, it's weird.

01:09:03.950 --> 01:09:05.700
It's something I learned
in Santo Domingo.

01:09:05.700 --> 01:09:08.229
It's a word that they don't
use too much in the United

01:09:08.229 --> 01:09:12.137
States is "simultaneity."

01:09:12.137 --> 01:09:13.970
So I'm always like,
fuck the "either" or the

01:09:13.970 --> 01:09:15.387
"or" because I
think you're right.

01:09:15.387 --> 01:09:17.045
But I think it's
good to do both.

01:09:17.045 --> 01:09:17.920
Get the young people.

01:09:17.920 --> 01:09:21.248
Get everybody every
age, but thank you.

01:09:21.248 --> 01:09:22.540
And I think you're quite right.

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:26.740
OK, I mean, and
that was the last--

01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:27.946
back there, those--

01:09:27.946 --> 01:09:29.279
AUDIENCE: I just had a question.

01:09:29.279 --> 01:09:31.080
DIAZ: Go ahead.

01:09:31.080 --> 01:09:33.670
AUDIENCE: Your second story
left me with some mixed feelings

01:09:33.670 --> 01:09:38.310
about interracial relationships,
and I wasn't quite sure

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:42.750
what you were trying to drive
home with that short story.

01:09:42.750 --> 01:09:46.125
Maybe I just wasn't
clear about that.

01:09:46.125 --> 01:09:48.640
DIAZ: Do you want to
elaborate your mixed feelings?

01:09:48.640 --> 01:09:51.500
AUDIENCE: Oh, it's that
I'm dating a white boy,

01:09:51.500 --> 01:09:53.200
and I'm Hispanic.

01:09:53.200 --> 01:09:56.760
And it made me wonder if maybe
I was going through a phase.

01:10:04.390 --> 01:10:05.830
DIAZ: God.

01:10:05.830 --> 01:10:07.143
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:07.143 --> 01:10:08.810
DIAZ: Somebody else
want to answer that?

01:10:14.637 --> 01:10:15.220
You know what?

01:10:18.310 --> 01:10:19.660
It's one of those things where--

01:10:22.880 --> 01:10:24.880
Again, I don't want to
get into personal issues.

01:10:27.670 --> 01:10:31.840
I'd rather just speak on
some old abstractness that

01:10:31.840 --> 01:10:34.870
might connect us in some way.

01:10:34.870 --> 01:10:39.380
I mean, again, nobody can tell
anybody who the fuck they can

01:10:39.380 --> 01:10:39.880
date.

01:10:39.880 --> 01:10:41.470
That's just the bottom line.

01:10:41.470 --> 01:10:42.970
I've never had any
issues with that,

01:10:42.970 --> 01:10:45.390
and I've never
worried about that.

01:10:45.390 --> 01:10:54.100
But I do find that
people pretend

01:10:54.100 --> 01:10:56.980
that when we talk about
racism, that racism is

01:10:56.980 --> 01:11:01.060
a bunch of just discrete little
units that are not connected,

01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:02.100
and I don't see that.

01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:05.800
I see it as a continuum,
so the kind of self-- and I

01:11:05.800 --> 01:11:07.375
never accuse anybody
of self-hatred

01:11:07.375 --> 01:11:08.500
because how would you know?

01:11:08.500 --> 01:11:11.710
You can't read minds, no
more than how can they know?

01:11:11.710 --> 01:11:13.545
Because they can't read
their subconscious.

01:11:13.545 --> 01:11:15.670
When somebody tells me that
they have no self-hate,

01:11:15.670 --> 01:11:17.760
I have to start laughing.

01:11:17.760 --> 01:11:21.170
I'm like, OK, you have total
access to your subconscious?

01:11:21.170 --> 01:11:23.858
I'm like, you need
to read [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:23.858 --> 01:11:24.400
Come on, now.

01:11:24.400 --> 01:11:26.200
This book's been
out for 30 years.

01:11:26.200 --> 01:11:29.320
But for me, what I find
interesting is some very

01:11:29.320 --> 01:11:31.810
simple things we were talking
about in another class

01:11:31.810 --> 01:11:36.040
where there's some very simple
patterns about desire and race.

01:11:36.040 --> 01:11:38.080
And the fact that
they are constant,

01:11:38.080 --> 01:11:39.700
that they're almost
constant, they're

01:11:39.700 --> 01:11:41.833
almost like a law
of physics, right?

01:11:41.833 --> 01:11:43.750
It was like, we were
joking with another class

01:11:43.750 --> 01:11:44.625
before I [INAUDIBLE].

01:11:44.625 --> 01:11:45.940
Some of the students are here.

01:11:45.940 --> 01:11:48.400
And me my boys when
we were coming up,

01:11:48.400 --> 01:11:51.250
we always notice that
no matter who we dated,

01:11:51.250 --> 01:11:55.040
they were always
lighter skinned than us.

01:11:55.040 --> 01:11:57.230
And then one of my friends
did this hilarious thing

01:11:57.230 --> 01:12:00.410
where he took every
single basketball player--

01:12:00.410 --> 01:12:03.440
and just watching ball because
he's an obsessive ball--

01:12:03.440 --> 01:12:06.270
and he just sat down with a
notebook for an entire season

01:12:06.270 --> 01:12:08.270
and discovered that like
every single basketball

01:12:08.270 --> 01:12:10.940
player dates somebody
or is married to someone

01:12:10.940 --> 01:12:13.160
lighter skinned than them.

01:12:13.160 --> 01:12:18.230
And the fact that it occurs with
such perfection and precision

01:12:18.230 --> 01:12:20.660
and without any
disruptions of it

01:12:20.660 --> 01:12:24.050
really speaks to
some wild-ass shit.

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:26.090
I mean, you couldn't
get that pattern

01:12:26.090 --> 01:12:29.247
unless you tried over time.

01:12:29.247 --> 01:12:30.830
And I guess that's
the sort of stuff I

01:12:30.830 --> 01:12:32.990
try to critique in my stories.

01:12:32.990 --> 01:12:36.530
Whether it has some relevance
to you personally, good luck.

01:12:36.530 --> 01:12:38.615
But I do think that
there is a lot of that,

01:12:38.615 --> 01:12:40.830
and there's a lot of--

01:12:40.830 --> 01:12:45.210
what we consider beauty
is some wild shit.

01:12:45.210 --> 01:12:47.730
It's culturally
pre-programmed, so I

01:12:47.730 --> 01:12:51.110
think it's good to
disrupt some of it.

01:12:51.110 --> 01:12:51.860
Whatever.

01:12:51.860 --> 01:12:56.600
I mean, I'm just putting an
intellectual spin on something

01:12:56.600 --> 01:13:00.650
that my friends in East Harlem
could say in two seconds.

01:13:00.650 --> 01:13:01.550
Fuck that.

01:13:04.910 --> 01:13:07.840
And you had that
question, and that's that?

01:13:07.840 --> 01:13:09.250
AUDIENCE: I had a question also.

01:13:09.250 --> 01:13:15.770
It seems like you pride yourself
on being Latino, Hispanic,

01:13:15.770 --> 01:13:17.370
black, and I'm not sure--

01:13:17.370 --> 01:13:18.800
Caribbean, Dominican.

01:13:18.800 --> 01:13:22.100
I was wondering where you think
the line should be drawn with

01:13:22.100 --> 01:13:26.110
being celebrating race and
singling someone out for race

01:13:26.110 --> 01:13:28.980
because sometimes it seems
like where in America,

01:13:28.980 --> 01:13:30.788
we call attention to
your race so much,

01:13:30.788 --> 01:13:33.080
sometimes people start seeing
[? others ?] [? before ?]

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:34.870
[? they'll ?] [? see ?] people.

01:13:34.870 --> 01:13:38.383
Like for example, with women,
if people see [INAUDIBLE] women

01:13:38.383 --> 01:13:40.800
[? write ?] women [? in ?]
[? this, ?] women [? in that ?]

01:13:40.800 --> 01:13:44.747
instead of saying, well, this is
writing, and then, we have men.

01:13:44.747 --> 01:13:45.330
We have women.

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:49.140
We have blacks, Latinos,
whites, whatever--

01:13:49.140 --> 01:13:51.015
I'm wondering where you
think the line should

01:13:51.015 --> 01:13:56.358
be drawn between celebrating
something and singling them

01:13:56.358 --> 01:13:58.310
out.

01:13:58.310 --> 01:14:00.720
DIAZ: Thank you.

01:14:00.720 --> 01:14:05.940
I mean, I come from a certain
philosophical bent where--

01:14:05.940 --> 01:14:09.720
forgive me, but like
I don't think others

01:14:09.720 --> 01:14:14.440
could be singled out anymore
than they already are.

01:14:14.440 --> 01:14:19.270
I think gender-blind
stuff and race-blind stuff

01:14:19.270 --> 01:14:21.040
is foolish nonsense.

01:14:21.040 --> 01:14:25.170
Our brains are hard-wired
to recognize difference.

01:14:25.170 --> 01:14:27.060
They just are.

01:14:27.060 --> 01:14:28.560
They are, and that's it.

01:14:28.560 --> 01:14:30.090
I think for me,
it's not a problem

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:31.500
with recognizing differences.

01:14:31.500 --> 01:14:33.810
It's describing all sorts
of negative and pejorative

01:14:33.810 --> 01:14:36.165
nonsense to it.

01:14:36.165 --> 01:14:37.540
And I think in
the States, people

01:14:37.540 --> 01:14:39.790
try to confuse those two.

01:14:39.790 --> 01:14:41.770
They're like, oh, we
shouldn't talk about race

01:14:41.770 --> 01:14:43.360
because that means that--

01:14:43.360 --> 01:14:46.170
I'm like, no.

01:14:46.170 --> 01:14:48.210
I don't mind being all
these multiple things.

01:14:48.210 --> 01:14:52.710
Just don't put negative
shit on it, and that's that.

01:14:52.710 --> 01:14:54.990
Just don't put some old
negative shit on it.

01:14:54.990 --> 01:14:58.625
And so those of us who want
to do that kind of work,

01:14:58.625 --> 01:15:00.500
I think that that's the
most important thing,

01:15:00.500 --> 01:15:03.920
and people feel really
uncomfortable with that.

01:15:03.920 --> 01:15:05.270
But I'm like, you know what?

01:15:05.270 --> 01:15:12.420
You should go-- you learn to
be humble when you travel,

01:15:12.420 --> 01:15:14.640
and no matter what you
say about yourself,

01:15:14.640 --> 01:15:18.030
people pick your ass out.

01:15:18.030 --> 01:15:19.110
I went to Japan.

01:15:19.110 --> 01:15:20.670
It was beyond.

01:15:20.670 --> 01:15:23.640
Nothing I could say about--
please don't select out.

01:15:23.640 --> 01:15:25.530
See me as a human being.

01:15:25.530 --> 01:15:26.490
That wasn't working.

01:15:31.590 --> 01:15:34.620
And for me the reality
is always the masses

01:15:34.620 --> 01:15:37.590
on some old weird shit.

01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:40.140
I mean, whenever I walk
in African communities,

01:15:40.140 --> 01:15:42.480
they always crack on me.

01:15:42.480 --> 01:15:45.720
They're always like,
yo, what are you, man?

01:15:45.720 --> 01:15:47.200
But do you know what I'm saying?

01:15:47.200 --> 01:15:47.700
So--

01:15:47.700 --> 01:15:50.556
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
what are you?

01:15:50.556 --> 01:15:54.840
I mean, [INAUDIBLE] do you
check "other" in the census box

01:15:54.840 --> 01:15:56.217
when you're filling those out?

01:15:56.217 --> 01:15:57.550
DIAZ: Oh, I don't check "other."

01:15:57.550 --> 01:15:59.160
AUDIENCE: Then
what do you check?

01:15:59.160 --> 01:16:01.760
DIAZ: Well, I always checked
that ugly word "Hispanic"

01:16:01.760 --> 01:16:05.430
till they changed it to
Latino, but I mean, again, it's

01:16:05.430 --> 01:16:09.280
like you have to do things.

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:14.140
For me, I think most of us
have multiple identities

01:16:14.140 --> 01:16:17.210
so that I don't mind
going through my list

01:16:17.210 --> 01:16:18.200
of multiple identities.

01:16:18.200 --> 01:16:20.030
If you're dumb enough
to ask, then you're

01:16:20.030 --> 01:16:22.100
dumb enough to have to listen.

01:16:22.100 --> 01:16:23.930
So I tend to be like,
it depends where

01:16:23.930 --> 01:16:26.198
the question is coming from.

01:16:26.198 --> 01:16:28.490
Where the question's coming
from is almost as important

01:16:28.490 --> 01:16:30.450
as the way you answer it.

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:32.130
So people will ask
me what you are,

01:16:32.130 --> 01:16:34.930
and if I don't like their
attitude, I'll be like,

01:16:34.930 --> 01:16:35.880
what am I?

01:16:35.880 --> 01:16:37.530
The fuck are you?

01:16:37.530 --> 01:16:40.920
That's the answer back to--

01:16:40.920 --> 01:16:47.550
and but the way, I mean, I see
it most clearly is I'm like,

01:16:47.550 --> 01:16:51.780
it's like a member of the
Dominican diaspora, which

01:16:51.780 --> 01:16:54.840
is a portion of the
larger African diaspora.

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:55.950
I'm an immigrant.

01:16:55.950 --> 01:17:00.555
I'm a Latino, and
I come from the--

01:17:00.555 --> 01:17:06.940
I'm a man, and I come from
the poor communities, urban.

01:17:06.940 --> 01:17:09.640
So you see the list grows,
but I don't mind that.

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:11.200
I think that's OK.

01:17:11.200 --> 01:17:14.330
All right, I'm just going to
read you guys just one page.

01:17:14.330 --> 01:17:14.830
I'm sorry.

01:17:14.830 --> 01:17:15.920
Y'all have to leave.

01:17:15.920 --> 01:17:16.420
Go ahead.

01:17:16.420 --> 01:17:18.330
You guys can go.

01:17:18.330 --> 01:17:23.218
No, I don't want to
put you on the spot.

01:17:23.218 --> 01:17:24.760
It's just, I just
wanted to leave you

01:17:24.760 --> 01:17:28.300
with just this one
little description

01:17:28.300 --> 01:17:31.360
from a story, the last
story that I'd published.

01:17:31.360 --> 01:17:34.570
And it's just this little piece
on the Dominican Republic,

01:17:34.570 --> 01:17:37.720
and it's just this
guy, this narrator

01:17:37.720 --> 01:17:39.130
is just talking
about how much he

01:17:39.130 --> 01:17:43.390
loves his country, which is this
sort of nonsense, but anyway.

01:17:43.390 --> 01:17:44.830
"Let me confess.

01:17:44.830 --> 01:17:46.930
I love Santo Domingo.

01:17:46.930 --> 01:17:49.420
I love coming home to the
guys in the blazers trying

01:17:49.420 --> 01:17:52.450
to push little cups of
Brugal into my hands.

01:17:52.450 --> 01:17:55.090
I love the plane landing,
everybody clapping when

01:17:55.090 --> 01:17:56.830
the wheels kiss the runway.

01:17:56.830 --> 01:17:59.500
I love the fact that I'm
the only nigga on board

01:17:59.500 --> 01:18:03.250
without a Cuban link or a
flapjack of makeup on my face.

01:18:03.250 --> 01:18:05.020
I love the redhead
woman on her way

01:18:05.020 --> 01:18:06.850
to meet her daughter
she hasn't seen

01:18:06.850 --> 01:18:09.730
in 11 years, the gifts
she holds in her laps

01:18:09.730 --> 01:18:11.340
like the bones of saints.

01:18:11.340 --> 01:18:15.040
[SPANISH] has [SPANISH]
now, the woman whispers.

01:18:15.040 --> 01:18:17.470
The last time I saw
her, she could barely

01:18:17.470 --> 01:18:20.180
speak in sentences,
and now she's a woman.

01:18:20.180 --> 01:18:22.090
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

01:18:22.090 --> 01:18:25.060
I love the bag my mother's
packed, shit for relatives

01:18:25.060 --> 01:18:27.640
and something for Magda, a gift.

01:18:27.640 --> 01:18:31.240
You give this to her
no matter what happens.

01:18:31.240 --> 01:18:33.460
If this was another
kind of story,

01:18:33.460 --> 01:18:35.590
I'd tell you about
the sea what it

01:18:35.590 --> 01:18:37.960
looked like, what it looks
like after it's been forced

01:18:37.960 --> 01:18:41.140
into the sky through a blowhole,
how when I'm driving in

01:18:41.140 --> 01:18:44.620
from the airport and see it
like this, like shredded silver,

01:18:44.620 --> 01:18:46.450
I know I'm back for real.

01:18:46.450 --> 01:18:49.210
I'd tell you how many poor
motherfuckers there are,

01:18:49.210 --> 01:18:51.580
more albinos, more
cross-eyed niggers,

01:18:51.580 --> 01:18:54.070
more [SPANISH] than
you'll ever see,

01:18:54.070 --> 01:18:57.070
and the [SPEAKING SPANISH].

01:18:57.070 --> 01:19:00.040
And I'd tell you about the
traffic, the entire history

01:19:00.040 --> 01:19:02.800
of late-20th-century
automobiles swimming

01:19:02.800 --> 01:19:05.000
across every flat
stretch of ground,

01:19:05.000 --> 01:19:07.930
a cosmology of battered
cars, battered motorcycles,

01:19:07.930 --> 01:19:10.150
battered trucks,
and battered buses,

01:19:10.150 --> 01:19:12.160
and an equal number
of repair shops,

01:19:12.160 --> 01:19:14.380
run by any idiot with a wrench.

01:19:14.380 --> 01:19:16.000
And I'd tell you
about the shanties

01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:18.910
and our no-running-water
faucets and the sambos

01:19:18.910 --> 01:19:21.790
on the billboards and the fact
that my family house comes

01:19:21.790 --> 01:19:24.520
equipped with the
ever-reliable latrine,

01:19:24.520 --> 01:19:26.440
and I'd tell you
about my [SPANISH]

01:19:26.440 --> 01:19:28.780
and his [SPANISH]
hands, how unhappy he is

01:19:28.780 --> 01:19:30.225
that I'm not sticking around.

01:19:30.225 --> 01:19:31.600
And I'd tell you
about the street

01:19:31.600 --> 01:19:35.350
where I was born, Calle XXI,
how it hasn't decided yet

01:19:35.350 --> 01:19:37.390
if it wants to be a
slum or not and how

01:19:37.390 --> 01:19:40.270
it's been in the state
of indecision for years.

01:19:40.270 --> 01:19:42.940
But that would make this
another kind of story,

01:19:42.940 --> 01:19:45.580
and I'm having trouble
as it is with this one.

01:19:45.580 --> 01:19:47.620
You'll have to take
my word for it.

01:19:47.620 --> 01:19:50.950
Santo Domingo is Santo
Domingo, and let's all pretend

01:19:50.950 --> 01:19:52.760
we know what goes on there."

01:19:52.760 --> 01:19:53.260
Thank you.

01:19:53.260 --> 01:19:55.110
Have a good night, you guys.

